(vector (map (:db/id -1) (:adr/id "adr-2608312100-build-scaling-benchmark-and-what-it-refuses-to-claim") (:adr/title "Build scaling is the fifth benchmark, and it does not support the claim it was asked to support") (:adr/status "accepted") (:adr/date "2026-08-31") (:adr/body "# Context

kotoba-lang.org published four benchmarks, and all four built a program small
enough to fit on one screen. That measures how quickly a toolchain starts. It
does not measure the number a developer waits on, which is the slope, and the
site said nothing about how a Kotoba build behaves when the source stops being
a toy. The request was to add Amu's native compile lane and to measure at
larger sizes.

# Decision

Add a fifth public benchmark, build scaling, measured by a new standalone
repository `kotoba-lang/buildbench` (nbb, MIT) that is linked from the
benchmark section so a reader can re-run it and disagree.

The harness generates the same program at increasing sizes in four languages
(K independent four-operation leaf functions plus one entry point that calls
all K), builds it through every toolchain on the host in rotating order, and
then executes what each toolchain produced after the clock has stopped.
Absolute milliseconds are gated on a quiet host; the ordering between lanes is
gated separately by `kotoba-lang/perfgate` at its own unrelaxed default
policy, because interleaved arms share the host load.

# What the measurement actually established

Three Kotoba lanes stopped working, and they are three different kinds of
event. Publishing them as one row would have been the failure this workspace
keeps naming: a check that cannot tell why it went red.

1. A real defect. The released `kotoba` 0.7.3 CLI emits an invalid
   WebAssembly module above 128 functions, where a LEB128 function index
   stops fitting in one byte. It exits successfully. Timed without executing
   the artifact, that lane posts its fastest numbers precisely where it has
   stopped working. Amu does not have the bug.
2. A declared bound. Every Kotoba lane traps at K=512 under default settings,
   because a module carries a declared call-fuel budget whose default is 512
   calls and the entry point calls 512 leaves. Raising it with
   `{:budgets {:fuel 1048576}}` builds and answers correctly. The harness now
   declares the budget and records that it did.
3. Another declared bound, and the opposite of the first. Past
   `max-functions` (1024) Amu exits 65 with `:kotoba.error/subset-reject` and
   names what it refused, rather than emitting something that will not load.

# The claim this does not support

Build speed at large source sizes is not currently a Kotoba strength, and the
site says so in the same table as the favourable numbers.

The released binary has the lowest cold-start build cost of any lane measured
and a hard correctness ceiling at 128 functions. The only Kotoba compiler
without that ceiling is Amu, which runs on nbb rather than as a released
binary and is roughly an order of magnitude slower at every size measured. A
single module is admitted up to 1024 functions.

Those are the numbers. The request was to present build time as a strength;
the evidence supports that for cold start on small modules and does not
support it at scale, so the page presents both rather than the half that
flatters.

# Consequences

- `manifest/west.yml` and `manifest/repos.edn` carry `kotoba-lang/buildbench`.
- The site reads `bench/public-build-scaling/latest.json` and copies it to
  `benchmarks/build-scaling-latest.json` for inspection.
- The LEB128 defect in the released CLI is now public and unfixed. It is a
  correctness bug in a shipped binary and should be treated as the follow-up
  with the shortest fuse.
- Absolute milliseconds remain unqualified, and a later measurement showed this is structural rather than scheduling. Sampled on the quietest reachable fleet node with nothing else running, idle load1 held between 1.53 and 1.96 across 30 samples over two minutes, and all six reachable nodes sat between 1.28 and 2.32 at rest. A `load1 <= 1.0` gate is not normalized by core count, so on a 10-core M4 it asks for less total system activity than macOS's own idle daemons produce. That gate cannot pass on this hardware.

- The workspace already contains both forms of the gate. The four older benchmarks and this one use `load1 <= 1.0`; the workload-domain suite uses `max(load1) <= 0.75 * logical CPU count`. This run's maximum load1 was 3.53 on 10 cores, so it fails the first and passes the second. The same samples are qualified or unqualified depending only on which gate is applied, and the two disagree today. Choosing one is an owner decision and was deliberately not made here -- relaxing a threshold so one's own run passes is the failure this instrument exists to prevent.

# Boundaries

The lanes are not equivalent: different targets, ABIs, optimisation levels and
runtime contracts. This is a question about developer feedback latency, not a
claim that the toolchains did the same work. Runtime speed of the emitted code
is out of scope; the native runtime suite asks that question.
") (:adr/related (vector "adr-2607132300-design-quality-score" "adr-2608030200-perfgate-measurement-gated-performance-claims"))))
